cious of the class structure that  was built into it but you were also  getting a pretty you know good education  you know we learned Latin in 6th and 7th  grade and then went on to another  language we were way ahead in  mathematics and science and so I was I  was getting some challenge  and we you know for our soccer coach we  had a former Olympian teaching us and so  I really enjoyed soccer so that was cool  but none of that had to do with the  teacher who I would say made the  greatest impact on me in middle school  and that was mrs. Becca Dale she and her  husband mr. Dale were and though maybe  he was mr. beacon I don't remember they  both work there  and she taught drama and she taught  English classes and we had to take drama  every single year as I remember 6th 7th  8th grade and you know we started out  with some some really interesting  exercise she was very good at what she  did and here's what made the biggest  impact on me so my dad found me writing  a play and it was not a very good play  at all what I look back at it it was  largely drawn from Lloyd Alexander's you  know chronicles of prettyand which is a  fantasy series for young adults about  this you know guy Taryn and had a lot of  sort the the play as I wrote it had a  lot of sword fighting in it a lot of  fighting the bad guys not a hell of a  lot of dialogue as I recall but I was in  the process of doing that my dad found  me do it doing that in my my room and he  said let me take a look at what you've  got there and he took it to mrs. bika  Dale and she said well this is pretty  good we're gonna put this on this is  going to be the play for our first  semester sixth grade and I didn't know  you know most of these other kids you  know but I got to know them in part  through being the guy who had written  the play and she would ask me well what  did you envision here what do you think  about this and of course I was much more  interested in you know having sword  fights and stuff like that but 