d he was for me and I think for many  other young men  Catholic Memorial and probably for a lot  of young women to sort of a model of  intelligence athletic and ethical  masculinity I remember the first day of  class gym class he put us all up on the  bleachers and he gave us a lecture red  in the face  you know shouting at us a triplets into  our head to impress upon us that we were  not to pull the bra straps of girls  anywhere in the school I guess this has  been a big problem the year before and  he wanted to stress to us that you know  there was a functional equivalence in  terms of pain and discomfort between  testicles and breasts and that if he  caught us doing anything remotely you  know untoward to any female students  with respect to their breasts we were in  deep trouble and and you could you know  you had the sense that this guy was very  serious about this and again it wasn't  about prudery it was about fairness and  trying to get us to think about other  people before we do stupid actions now  of course all of us were studying it's  like what the hell is this guy yelling  at us but you know he was he was kind of  an unusual gym teacher at the time many  gym teachers back then were kind of  jerks they were very macho very  sometimes even sadistic you know  encouraging you to use any means  necessary to win Bovo was all about  fairness and and there wasn't any sort  of like well it's okay if you if you  lost because you played the game right  he didn't go that far  which he shouldn't but he stressed that  it was important to actually follow the  rules and do things the right way even  today when I when I workout I sort of I  don't hear his voice in my head but I  but I these words keep coming up to me  don't cheat yourself and that was an  important aspect to it wasn't just don't  cheat the other people don't cheat the  coach don't cheat the game don't cheat  yourself don't deprive your  self of what discipline would would  actually make possible for you if you're  doing you know a rep do the rep all the  way the right way if you're running you  know and you've got to do eight 100-yard  dashes do every single one of those the  right way and if you if you fail on one  do it again and and you know I found  this was so important for me outside of  the field of athletics that mentality is  what allowed me to learn foreign  languages the way that I did this you  know needing to drill and drill and  drill I I learned that more from I would  say from Chuck bova than from from  anybody else and and I was learning it  in a different context so you know that  was he was another very important  teacher another one that I can think of  is Robert reuleaux people call them Bob  but you know he was actually very formal  I had him for creative writing my first  semester he would say you know quite  biting sarcastic things in class but  he's a very good writing teacher and  then I had him again for British  literature and he was the kind of guy  who you could tell was was quite serious  about his his subject matter and he you  know he could sometimes be a bit of a  martinet you know and the chickens are  done people are finished sort of stuff  but I remember coming to him after I had  you know graduated from college gone and  graduated from high school gone into the  army come out I was writing some poetry  and bringing I brought the poetry to him  and I remember he asked me tough  questions about he I said what do you  think about it he said now this stuff  isn't very good and I was sort of  crestfallen all right and and then he  went on to tell me why it wasn't very  good this point this point this point  and I remember very well I had a line in  there about you know sort of losing my  philosophy meaning sort of losing my  sense of where I was going in life and I  remember him asking me the question why  should that matter not just to you but  to the reader why should they care at  all about whether you've lost your  fear not and he wasn't saying it to me  to brush me off he was saying it to me  to try to provoke me into actually  thinking and then writing something a  bit more fully to articulate myself the  other person who made a massive impact  on me and and probably is the one who's  responsible for me having a  philosophical vocation in the first  place I don't even know his first name  he was a substitute teacher who came in  for one semester from Seattle mr.  Lorenzo and he taught a sacraments class  and he taught st. Augustine to us he  said he replaced a nun who would have  probably been terrible as a teacher and  he said Augustine you know changed my  life I converted because of him and  reading his confessions and going  through you know what I'd gone through  in Seattle I want to introduce him to  you he is the saint for sinners and he  talks about the sacraments and then he  said now before I can actually teach you  about st. Augustine I'm gonna have to  explain some Plato to you because Plato  is very important and then we need  Aristotle as sort of a counterpoint to  to that and then we can talk about the  manna Kea you know Manichaean religion  that that Augustine was in for a while  and then we can talk about Augustine and  we actually had a little bit of time  left at the end of the semester so he  was like you know why you've seen it was  a psychologist let's talk about Freud  Jung and Adler so he taught in a way  radically different than any of the  other teachers you know there was no  rote memorization we didn't have a  textbook he would you know put stuff up  on the chalkboard and then we would talk  about it and we'd ask questions most of  the other students hated it because they  had no idea what was coming up on the  exam and they they viewed his classes  unnecessarily you know confusing and  difficult I thrived in it and I was  getting to see what philosophy could  really be like because I had a  philosophy class at Catholic Memorial  and it was pretty bad it you know it was  the sort of thing that had that been my  only experience with philosophy would  have told me I  I never wanted to go into that field so  mr. Lorenzo another very important  teacher in my my high school career then  I went to Lakeland College which is now  Lakeland University and there were there  were three professors who really made a  big impact on me  interestingly none of them were  philosophy professors instead they  thought other stuff there was Lucretia  Crawford who was my writing instructor  in freshman year we had to take  expository and persuasive writing I was  a good writer and she more or less  allowed me to do what I wanted to do but  I had to keep within certain guidelines  and you know when I think about the work  that I made for her you know in  persuasive writing we had to eventually  culminate in writing a research paper it  was supposed to be about like 10 to 20  pages or so I wrote a 70 page paper  double-spaced you know and I wish I  still had it you know to take a look at  it and it was essentially about whether  television was a good or bad thing  that's kind of a big topic I was doing a  lot of research and I made the case that  that in many respects television was  actually a bad thing but it could be  harnessed in different ways she actually  read that entire paper and gave me  feedback on it she let me write the  paper in the first place I mean a lot of  professors you know a student you know  who's got a paper that's spiraling out  of control they'd say rein it in buddy  I'm not reading that that stuff you know  but she she understood that that was  something that I had within me and could  do so she she she encouraged me and then  later I one of the best classes I ever  took from her was a a winterim class we  had this this special winter session  where you'd meet with with you know for  a month three hours a day five days a  week and we read our ways through the  Arthurian literature and boy did she  come alive I mean s