ow he was sort of a model again of  competency and you know interest in his  his subject  he was also rather you know sardonic guy  in many respects perhaps that might have  rubbed off on me a little bit at the  time I think it probably had a good  nerve of that going already but he was  he was a really interesting guy to  interact with and then you know the last  professor that I'll talk about I didn't  actually take any classes with and he's  retiring this year from Lakeland College  his name is Mara bond Kota Von D and  he's Iranian he taught in psychology and  education and I never did take any  classes with him although he did give a  guest lecture on Zoroastrianism in our  comparative religion class at one time  but I interacted with him all the time  and you know we would chat about this  and that and and I sometimes go into his  office and sometimes we'd you know meet  up at events and he was just such a  good-hearted genial guy I'll tell you  something else about him that that's  really touched me quite a bit so I've  been gone from this area here Wisconsin  for 20 years and I recently you know  moved back here with my wife who's also  from the area and Lakeland College asked  me to give a think house talk on anger  and what we can do with it and that  involved me going up to campus several  times and while I was there I went in  Old Main where the faculty offices are  and I you know I was walking around  seeing who's there who's not metal it  met up with a couple other professors  who I thought were pretty cool  and there was mayor bond co2 Von Teese  office so I I went over there and I said  I wonder if he's gonna even know who I  am and I walked in and he looked at me  for a minute now this was  graduated in 1994 so this is more than  20 years ago he looked at me and many he  said oh how are you doing Greg he'd  remembered our conversations that we had  you know when I was you know some sort  of bright but in many respects probably  kind of naive and you