 to  taking his classes and that's where he  put a lot of his attention so I was very  sad to see him go  the another professor who made a huge  impact on me at the time and and  probably even still down until today it  was eventually became my mentor  Garth Gillen and Garth actually by the  time that he left Southern Illinois  University had three advanced degrees  one in philosophy one in theology and  one in educational psychology when he  retired he opened up a clinical practice  and is still doing it today as far as I  know we've actually I haven't been in  touch for quite some time his classes  were very exciting because he taught  contemporary continental philosophy and  mostly French stuff some German stuff he  was very big on Adorno he actually  taught a class too on Karl Rahner  believe it or not but he'd mostly do  things like you know teaching dari da or  you know Marilyn Paul tearful call or  people like that and his approach to the  texts was intensely hermeneutical we  would take the text and try to figure  out what what is going on in this  passage what are all the reference  points and and we would sort and then we  bring it back to whether any of this was  actually true he was also very  interested in Jacques Lacan he's he's  one of the people who got me interested  in him but he always stressed the need  to figure out how much of this is  [ __ ] how much of this is actually  applicable and usable and so he was he  was he was also kind of an upstart you  know he was a Catholic Marxist from way  back he'd grown up very blue-collar out  in Montana  so there were a lot of ways in which we  could relate and is an interesting guy  who's kind of on the outs with the  department one when I first got there  and I was actually his last doctoral  student he retired shortly before I was  able to finish my dissertation so I had  a co-chair but he was officially the  chair then the third person who really  made a big impact on me without really  meaning to do s